<article class="env-corollary" id="00HW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00HW">Corollary <span data-tag="00HW">6.2.4</span>.</a> Let $\mathbb {C}[G]$ be the group algebra of $G$. Define the linear map $A\to \mathbb {C}[G]$ by </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  [P_ g]\mapsto \sum _{h \in G} hgh^{-1}.  \]
</div>
  <p> Then this map is an algebra isomorphism $A\xrightarrow {\sim }\mathbb {C}[G]^ G=z(\mathbb {C}[G])$ (here, $z$ stands for the center of the algebra), which identifies $\varepsilon $ with the map </p>
  <div class="equation" id="00EP">
    <span class="equation-label"><a href="/tag/00EP" data-tag="00EP">6.2.10</a></span>
    <div>\begin{equation} \label{e:dw-eps} \varepsilon (g) = \frac{1}{|G|}\delta _{g, 1}= \frac{1}{|G|^2}\operatorname{tr}_{R}(g), \end{equation}</div>
</div>
  <p> where $R=\mathbb {C}[G]$ is the regular representation. </p>
</article>